COMMAND NAME: mpprebuildcluster.sh

Rebuilds a master using a SQL script file containing the Greenplum Database 
schema and Greenplum system catalog information, and then recreates all 
of the segment instances using the information found in the Greenplum 
system catalog tables.


*****************************************************
SYNOPSIS
*****************************************************

mpprebuildcluster.sh -s sql_file -d temp_db_data_directory [-p 
temp_db_port] [-m] [-o] [-i] [-c checkpoint_segments] [-x] [-i 
cidr_address] [-n locale] [-a] [-q] [-l logfile_directory] [-D] 
[-? | -v]


*****************************************************
DESCRIPTION
*****************************************************

The mpprebuildcluster.sh script will rebuild a Greenplum Database system 
using a SQL script file. This SQL script file must contain commands for 
creating the Greenplum Database schema and loading system catalog data from 
another Greenplum Database installation. There are two ways to get the SQL 
script file that mpprebuildcluster.sh needs:

* mpp_dump  The mpp_dump operation creates a file on the master host called 
  mpp_catalog_1_<dbid>_<timestamp>.

* pg_dump  PostgreSQL provides a utility called pg_dump, which backs up a 
  single PostgreSQL database. You can use this utility to backup the 
  pg_catalog schema of the master host, which contains the system 
  catalog tables. For example, run the following command on the master host:
  pg_dump -n pg_catalog -p 5432 template1

The use of the mpprebuildcluster.sh script assumes that you have an existing 
or previous Greenplum Database system that you want to recreate. By default 
this script uses the same hosts, ports, and data directories as the 
reference Greenplum Database system. You can use the -o option to 
reconfigure the system to a new master host. 

If you want to alter any other information in the system configuration 
(such as segment hosts, data directory locations, or ports) use the -m option.
This option will create the Greenplum master instance only. After the 
Greenplum master is created, you can manually edit the Greenplum system 
catalog tables to change the system configuration. 
(see the Greenplum Database Administrator Guide) After editing the system 
catalog tables, use the mpprebuildseg.sh script, which rebuilds 
the segment instances using the information found in the Greenplum 
masters system catalog tables.

The use of this script also assumes that you have the same number of 
segment instances as the reference system you are rebuilding. If you are 
expanding your system or are dramatically changing your system configuration, 
you should use mppcreatecluster.sh to create a new Greenplum Database 
system from scratch.


*****************************************************
OPTIONS
*****************************************************

-s <schema_sql_file>

Required. A SQL file containing the Greenplum Database schema and system 
catalog information. This is the mpp_catalog_0_<dbid>_<timestamp> file 
created on the Greenplum master host by the mpp_dump operation. You can 
also do a pg_dump operation on a Greenplum master host to get a SQL 
file that can be used by mpprebuildcluster.sh. For example:
pg_dump -n pg_catalog -p 5432 template1


-d <temp_db_data_directory>

Required. The rebuild script creates a temporary database in this location. 
The temporary database is used to parse the contents of the SQL script file, 
and is then deleted after the rebuild operation is complete. Choose a 
different data storage location than the one used by your production 
Greenplum master instance.


-p <temp_db_port>

Optional. If not specified, the default port of 55555 is used. The rebuild 
script starts the temporary database using this port. The temporary database 
is used to parse the contents of the SQL script file, and is then deleted 
after the rebuild operation is complete. Choose a different port than the 
one used by your production Greenplum master instance.


-m (master only)

Optional. This option creates the Greenplum master instance only (the 
Greenplum Database schema and system catalog), but does not create the 
segment instances as specified in the system catalog. The default behavior 
is to rebuild all segment instances as they existed when the backup file 
was created.

Use this option if you want to change any information in your system 
configuration (such as segment hosts, data storage locations, or ports) 
before rebuilding the segments. After the Greenplum master is created, 
you can manually edit the system catalog tables to change the configuration. 
After editing the system catalog tables, use the mpprebuildseg.sh script, 
which rebuilds the segment instances using the information found in 
the Greenplum masters system catalog tables.


-o (use current host as master host)

Optional. Use this option if you are rebuilding the Greenplum Database 
system using a different master host than the one from which the backup 
SQL file was created. This option will override the master hostname found 
in the system catalog and set it to the current host name.


-i (start synchronization)

If a backup master host is found in the system catalog configuration, 
initialize the backup master synchronization process after rebuilding 
the backup master host.


-a (do not prompt)

Do not prompt the user for confirmation.


-q (no screen output)

Run in quiet mode. Command output is not displayed on the screen, but is 
still written to the log file.


-x (use cdatabase file)

Do not create the database if cdatabase file exists in same directory as 
the SQL source file.


-i cidr_address

Alternative IP or network address for the pg_hba.conf file, which determines 
the remote hosts allowed to connect to a Greenplum master instance. The 
default is 0.0.0.0/0.


- n <locale>

Sets the default locale (character set encoding) for the Greenplum Database 
system. Defaults to en_US.utf8 (UNICODE). You must make sure that the 
locale you specify exists on your operating system (locale -a will show 
which locales are available). See 'Character Set Support' in the PostgreSQL 
documentation for more information on the supported character set encodings.


-l <logfile_directory>

The directory to write the log file. Defaults to ~/mppAdminLogs.


-D (debug)

Sets logging level to debug.


-? (help)

Displays the online help.


-v (show script version)

Displays the version, status, last updated date, and check sum of this script.


*****************************************************
EXAMPLES
*****************************************************

Rebuild an entire Greenplum Database system (master and segment instances) 
using the exact same array configuration as in the SQL backup file:

mpprebuildcluster.sh -s mpp_catalog_0_1_2005103112453 -d /home/gpadmin/tmp


Rebuild an entire Greenplum Database system (master and segment instances) 
using the same array configuration as in the SQL backup file, but 
change the master host instance configuration to the current host:

mpprebuildcluster.sh -s mpp_catalog_0_1_2005103112453 -d /home/gpadmin/tmp -o


Rebuild the master host only (the Greenplum Database system catalog):

mpprebuildcluster.sh -s mpp_catalog_0_1_2005103112453 -d /home/gpadmin/tmp -o -m


